.Dd July 27, 2026
.Dt SECUREDKIM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm securedkim
.Nd DKIM signing and verification milter for Postfix
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl c Ar config-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a high-performance DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) milter implementing
RFC 6376 and RFC 8463 (Ed25519-SHA256).
It integrates with Postfix via the Sendmail milter protocol version 6,
verifying DKIM signatures on incoming mail and optionally signing outgoing
mail.
.Pp
.Nm
supports RSA-SHA256 and Ed25519-SHA256 algorithms, multi-listener
configurations with independent modes per listener, and SigningTable/KeyTable
for multi-domain signing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c Ar config-file
Path to the INI-style configuration file.
Required.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file uses INI format with a
.Ql [global]
section and one or more
.Ql [listener:name]
sections.
.Ss Global Section
.Bl -tag -width "SyslogFacility" -compact
.It Cm AuthservID
Identifier for Authentication-Results headers.
Default:
.Dq localhost .
.It Cm StripAuthResults
Remove every pre-existing Authentication-Results header claiming our
.Cm AuthservID ,
not just the ones asserting a
.Li dkim=
result.
Enable this only when
.Nm
is the first milter in the chain (RFC 8601 Section 5); with
.Xr securespf 8
in front of it, set the option there instead.
Default: no.
.It Cm WorkerThreads
Number of worker threads.
0 means auto-detect CPU count.
Default: 0.
.It Cm PidFile
Path to the PID file.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/securedkim/securedkim.pid .
.It Cm Foreground
Run in foreground without daemonizing.
Default: no.
.It Cm User
Drop privileges to this user after binding sockets.
.It Cm Syslog
Enable syslog output.
Default: yes.
.It Cm SyslogFacility
Syslog facility.
Default: mail.
.It Cm LogLevel
Logging verbosity: err, warn, info, debug.
Default: info.
.It Cm DnsNameserver
Comma-separated DNS nameserver IPs.
Default:
.Dq 127.0.0.1 .
.It Cm DnsTimeout
DNS query timeout in seconds.
Default: 5.
.It Cm DnsRetries
Number of DNS query retries.
Default: 2.
.It Cm DnsCacheSize
Maximum DNS cache entries per worker.
Default: 1000.
.It Cm DnsNegativeTTL
Negative cache TTL in seconds.
Default: 60.
.It Cm SignedHeaders
Colon-separated list of headers to include in signatures.
Default:
.Dq from:to:subject:date:message-id .
.It Cm MaxBodyBytes
Largest message body buffered in order to hash it.
Accepts an optional
.Cm K , Cm M
or
.Cm G
suffix.
Default: 10M.
0 disables the limit.
.Pp
Should be at least the MTA's
.Va message_size_limit .
A body over the limit is neither hashed nor silently truncated: verification
reports
.Cm dkim=temperror
and signing is skipped, because a body hash taken over a partial copy would
describe a message the MTA is not delivering.
.It Cm MaxHeaders
Largest number of headers accumulated for one message.
Default: 500.
0 disables the limit.
.It Cm MaxHeaderBytes
Largest total header size, names plus values, accumulated for one message.
Accepts a size suffix.
Default: 1M.
0 disables the limit.
.Pp
A message exceeding either header limit is tempfailed rather than delivered.
Headers past the limit are never read, and
.Nm
can only strip a forged
.Cm Authentication-Results
header that it read, so delivering the message would let one hidden beyond the
limit through uninspected.
Raise these if legitimate mail crossing many forwarders and mailing lists is
being deferred.
.It Cm MaxSignatures
Largest number of
.Cm DKIM-Signature
headers verified in one message.
Default: 20, matching OpenDKIM's option of the same name.
0 disables the limit.
.Pp
Each signature costs a DNS lookup and an RSA verify, so an unbounded count is
remote CPU and DNS amplification.
Past the limit no signature is verified and the result is
.Cm dkim=permerror .
.Pp
.Cm MaxBodyBytes , Cm MaxHeaders , Cm MaxHeaderBytes
and
.Cm MaxSignatures
are read at startup only.
.Dv SIGHUP
does not change them.
.It Cm MinimumKeyBits
Smallest RSA key, in bits, accepted from a signer's DNS key record.
Default: 1024.
.Pp
Unlike the
.Cm Max Ns -prefixed
limits above, 0 does not disable this check.
RFC 8301 \[sc]3.2 states that verifiers
.Dq MUST NOT consider signatures using RSA keys of less than 1024 bits as valid signatures ,
so a configured value below 1024 is raised to 1024 and a warning is logged.
A 512-bit modulus is factorable for a few hundred dollars of rented compute,
which would make
.Cm dkim=pass
meaningless while DMARC continued to treat it as an authenticated identity.
.Pp
A signature whose key is below the limit is reported as
.Cm dkim=permerror
with the reason
.Dq key too small ,
matching RFC 8301's statement that such signatures
.Dq have permanently failed evaluation .
A warning naming the signing domain is logged as well, since the fault is the
signer's and the reason cannot otherwise be told apart from a canonicalization
failure.
.Pp
Raising this to 2048 matches the RFC's
.Sy SHOULD
for signers, at the cost of a
.Cm permerror
for any correspondent still publishing a 1024-bit key, which remains legal.
.Pp
This option governs only keys published by others.
The signing key configured with
.Cm SignKeyFile
or through
.Cm KeyTable
is always held to the 1024-bit floor irrespective of this setting, and the
daemon refuses to start if it is smaller: RFC 8301 binds signers to the same
minimum, so mail signed below it fails DKIM at every conformant verifier.
Read at startup only.
.It Cm BodyLengthTag
What a verified signature's
.Cm l=
tag means.
One of
.Cm honor
or
.Cm refuse .
Default:
.Cm honor .
An unrecognised value is refused at startup rather than silently falling back.
.Pp
The
.Cm l=
tag states how many octets of the canonicalized body a signature covers, leaving
anything beyond that count unsigned.
It exists so that a mailing list which appends a footer, and does not re-sign,
does not thereby break the original signature.
.Pp
With
.Cm honor ,
the tag is obeyed as RFC 6376 \[sc]3.5 specifies: only the first
.Cm l=
octets are hashed.
A signature claiming to cover more body than the message actually has is refused
as
.Cm dkim=permerror ,
since RFC 6376 requires that the value
.Dq MUST NOT be larger than the actual number of octets in the canonicalized message body .
When a signature passes but leaves a tail uncovered, a warning naming the
signing domain and the number of unsigned octets is logged, because
.Cm dkim=pass
on its own cannot express that only part of the body was vouched for.
.Pp
With
.Cm refuse ,
a signature carrying
.Cm l=
is not evaluated at all and is reported as
.Cm dkim=policy ,
which RFC 8601 \[sc]2.7.1 defines as
.Dq the message was signed but the signature or signatures were not acceptable to the verifier .
Signatures without
.Cm l=
are unaffected.
.Pp
The reason to consider
.Cm refuse
is RFC 6376 \[sc]8.2, which observes that an intermediary can append content
after the signed portion, that the appended content may
.Dq completely replace the original content in the end recipient's eyes ,
and that the signature still validates while it does.
The same section concludes that
.Dq Assessors might wish to ignore signatures that use the tag .
Both settings are therefore standards-conformant; the choice is whether
interoperability with such lists is worth accepting a partially signed body.
.Cm l=
is rare in practice and RFC 6377 \[sc]3.5 discourages its use.
Read at startup only.
.It Cm ZmqEndpoint
ZeroMQ PUB endpoint for event publishing.
Disabled if not set.
.It Cm ZmqTopic
ZeroMQ topic prefix.
Default:
.Dq dkim .
.El
.Ss Listener Sections
Each
.Ql [listener:name]
section defines a socket and its operating mode.
.Bl -tag -width "SigningTable" -compact
.It Cm Socket
Listen address.
Format:
.Dq inet:port@ip
or
.Dq unix:/path .
The
.Ar ip
must be a numeric IPv4 or IPv6 address; no name resolution is performed.
A value that cannot be parsed is a fatal configuration error and is reported
with the section that contains it \(em it is never ignored, and never falls
back to the default listener.
.It Cm Mode
Operating mode for
.Em this listener :
.Dq sign ,
.Dq verify ,
or
.Dq both .
A listener that does not set
.Cm Mode
inherits
.Cm Mode
from the
.Ql [global]
section, or
.Dq verify
if that is unset.
Any other value is a configuration error and
.Nm
refuses to start.
.Pp
Each listener is independent.
Releases before this one held a single daemon-wide mode that every
.Ql [listener:...]
section overwrote in turn, so the last section in the file decided the mode for
every socket.
This mattered most when a
.Dq sign
section appeared below a
.Dq verify
section, as in the sample configuration: the public inbound socket then ran in
sign mode, and inbound mail whose author domain matched the signing
configuration was given a signature under this host's own key.
Check the
.Ql listener[N] mode=...
lines logged at startup against the intended configuration after upgrading.
.It Cm SigningTable
Path to signing table file mapping sender patterns to signing identities.
Required for sign mode with multi-domain support.
.It Cm KeyTable
Path to key table file mapping signing identities to key files.
Required for sign mode with multi-domain support.
.It Cm Domain
Single-domain signing shorthand: domain name.
.It Cm Selector
Single-domain signing shorthand: DKIM selector.
.It Cm KeyFile
Single-domain signing shorthand: path to private key file.
.El
.Pp
If no listener sections are defined, the default is
.Dq inet:8891@127.0.0.1
in verify mode, loopback only.
.Pp
The milter protocol carries no authentication, so any client that can reach a
listener is trusted completely.
On a
.Cm sign
listener this is an unauthenticated signing oracle: the caller can have arbitrary
mail signed under the configured domain and selector.
On a
.Cm verify
listener the caller dictates the
.Va Authentication-Results
field this host records.
Only the local MTA should ever connect.
Bind a routable address only when the MTA runs on a different machine, and
restrict access to it with
.Xr pf 4 .
.Ss Signing Table Format
One entry per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
*@example.com    signing-identity-name
.Ed
.Ss Key Table Format
One entry per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
signing-identity-name    domain:selector:/path/to/key.pem
.Ed
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGTERM" -compact
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reload configuration (including key tables).
.It Dv SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown with 30 second drain timeout.
.El
.Sh POSTFIX INTEGRATION
Typical setup with separate verify and sign instances:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Inbound verification
smtpd_milters = inet:127.0.0.1:8891

# Outbound signing (in submission or transport)
smtp_milters = inet:127.0.0.1:8892

milter_connect_macros = j {daemon_name} v {client_addr}
milter_default_action = accept
.Ed
.Sh LOG FORMAT
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securedkim[pid]: id=QUEUEID peer=HOST[IP] client=IP from=ADDR listener=N mode=verify|sign|both elapsed=Nms
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/local/etc/securedkim/securedkim.conf"
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/securedkim/securedkim.conf
Default configuration file.
.It Pa /var/run/securedkim/securedkim.pid
Default PID file.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr securedkim-genkey 8 ,
.Xr securedkim-testkey 8 ,
.Xr securespf 8 ,
.Xr securedmarc 8 ,
.Xr securearc 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Signatures
.%R RFC 6376
.Re
.Rs
.%T Use of RSA-SHA-256 and Ed25519-SHA-256 in DKIM
.%R RFC 8463
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Morante
